{"content":[{"signature":"","thinking":"\nLet me analyze this task carefully. I need to implement a `solve(case)` function that performs grammar-guided node reduction on a parse tree.\n\nKey rules:\n1. Consider deeper nodes before shallower ones (process deepest first)\n2. Never remove root, required symbols, or nodes with retained children\n3. Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\n4. Use node ID as tie-breaker at equal depth\n5. Return sorted retained IDs and actual removal order\n\nLet me think through the algorithm:\n\n1. Build the tree structure from the nodes\n2. Process nodes from deepest to shallowest (stable tie-break by node ID)\n3. For each candidate node (removable=True), check:\n   - It's not the root\n   - Its symbol is not a required symbol... wait, \"never remove a required symbol\" - does this mean a node whose symbol is in required_symbols?\n   - It has no retained children\n   - After removal, all required symbols remain represented\n\nWait, let me re-read: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\"\n\nSo \"a required symbol\" means a node whose symbol is in `required_symbols`? Or does it mean the symbol itself can't be removed?\n\nLooking at the fixture: required_symbols = [\"root\", \"s1\"]. The root node has symbol \"root\" and removable=false. Nodes n1 (s1, removable=false), n4 (s1, removable=true), n6 (s0, removable=true)...\n\nSo n4 has symbol \"s1\" which is in required_symbols. n4 is removable=true. But \"never remove a required symbol\" would mean we can't remove n4.\n\nWait, but also \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" This means even if a node's symbol isn't required, if removing it would mean no node with a required symbol remains, we can't remove it.\n\nLet me think more carefully:\n\nFor each node being considered for removal:\n1. It must be `removable=true`\n2. Its symbol must not be in `required_symbols` (or... hmm)\n3. It must have no retained children\n4. After removal, all required symbols must still be represented in the remaining tree\n\nActually, I think \"never remove a required symbol\" means: if a node's symbol is in `required_symbols`, never remove it. AND \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" is an additional check.\n\nLet me look at the nodes again for case 0:\n- root: symbol=root, removable=false\n- n1: symbol=s1, removable=false\n- n2: symbol=s2, removable=true\n- n3: symbol=s0, removable=false\n- n4: symbol=s1, removable=true (depth=2)\n- n5: symbol=s2, removable=false (depth=2)\n- n6: symbol=s0, removable=true (depth=2)\n\nrequired_symbols = [\"root\", \"s1\"]\n\nProcessing order (deepest first, then by node ID):\n- depth=2: n4, n5, n6 (sorted by ID: n4, n5, n6)\n- depth=1: n1, n2, n3\n- depth=0: root\n\nFor n4 (symbol=s1, removable=true):\n- removable: yes\n- symbol s1 is in required_symbols → cannot remove\n\nFor n5 (symbol=s2, removable=false):\n- removable: no → skip\n\nFor n6 (symbol=s0, removable=true):\n- removable: yes\n- symbol s0 not in required_symbols\n- no children\n- after removal: s1 still represented by n1 and n4 (wait, if n4 is retained)... yes, s1 still represented\n- → remove n6\n\nAfter removing n6, retained nodes: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5\n\nNow depth=1: n1, n2, n3\n\nFor n1 (symbol=s1, removable=false):\n- removable: no → skip\n\nFor n2 (symbol=s2, removable=true):\n- removable: yes\n- symbol s2 not in required_symbols\n- children: n4. n4 is still retained → n2 has retained children → cannot remove\n\nFor n3 (symbol=s0, removable=false):\n- removable: no → skip\n\nSo after processing, removed = [n6], retained = [root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5]\n\nHmm wait, but if n2 can't be removed because n4 is retained, and n4 can't be removed because its symbol is s1 (required)... n2 stays.\n\nLet me think about this more carefully. The algorithm:\n\nProcess nodes in order: deepest first, ties broken by node ID (lexicographic or numeric?).\n\nNode IDs are \"root\", \"n1\", \"n2\", etc. Lexicographic order would be: n1 \u003c n2 \u003c n3 \u003c n4 \u003c n5 \u003c n6 \u003c root.\n\nActually for tie-breaking at equal depth, we sort by node ID. Let me use lexicographic string comparison.\n\nFor depth 2: n4, n5, n6 - lexicographic order: n4, n5, n6 ✓\n\nAlgorithm:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    \n    # Validate required fields\n    for node in nodes:\n        for field in [\"id\", \"parent\", \"symbol\", \"depth\", \"removable\", \"utility\"]:\n            if field not in node:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Missing field {field}\")\n    \n    # Build structures\n    node_map = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    \n    # Find root\n    roots = [n for n in nodes if n[\"parent\"] is None]\n    if len(roots) != 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"Expected exactly one root node\")\n    \n    for node in nodes:\n        if node[\"parent\"] is not None:\n            if node[\"parent\"] not in node_map:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Parent {node['parent']} not found\")\n            children[node[\"parent\"]].append(node[\"id\"])\n    \n    # Track retained set\n    retained = set(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes)\n    removal_order = []\n    \n    # Sort nodes: deepest first, then by node ID (lexicographic)\n    # We process in this order\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n    \n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node[\"id\"]\n        \n        # Skip if not removable\n        if not node[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        \n        # Skip if symbol is required\n        if node[\"symbol\"] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        \n        # Skip if has retained children\n        if any(child in retained for child in children[nid]):\n            continue\n        \n        # Check if removing this node still preserves all required symbols\n        # (all required symbols must still be represented)\n        test_retained = retained - {nid}\n        symbols_after = {node_map[n][\"symbol\"] for n in test_retained}\n        \n        if not required_symbols.issubset(symbols_after):\n            continue\n        \n        # Remove the node\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        removal_order.append(nid)\n    \n    # Return results\n    return {\n        \"retained_ids\": sorted(retained),\n        \"removal_order\": removal_order\n    }\n```\n\nWait, but \"sorted retained IDs\" - sorted how? Lexicographic? Let me use lexicographic sort.\n\nLet me trace through the cases:\n\nCase 0:\n- Nodes sorted by (-depth, id):\n  - depth 2: n4, n5, n6\n  - depth 1: n1, n2, n3\n  - depth 0: root\n\nProcessing n4 (symbol=s1, required) → skip\nProcessing n5 (removable=false) → skip\nProcessing n6 (symbol=s0, removable=true):\n  - no children\n  - test_retained = {root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5}\n  - symbols = {root, s1, s2, s0, s1, s2} = {root, s1, s2, s0}\n  - required_symbols = {root, s1} ⊆ symbols → OK\n  - Remove n6, removal_order = [n6]\n\nProcessing n1 (removable=false) → skip\nProcessing n2 (symbol=s2, removable=true):\n  - children of n2: [n4]\n  - n4 in retained? Yes → has retained children → skip\n\nProcessing n3 (removable=false) → skip\nProcessing root (removable=false) → skip\n\nResult: retained = {root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5}, sorted = [n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, root]\nremoval_order = [n6]\n\nHmm, but lexicographic sort: \"n1\" \u003c \"n2\" \u003c \"n3\" \u003c \"n4\" \u003c \"n5\" \u003c \"root\"? Yes, because 'n' \u003c 'r'.\n\nActually wait, I need to double-check the sort. In Python, string comparison is lexicographic. \"n1\" vs \"root\": 'n' \u003c 'r', so n1 \u003c root. So sorted order would be: n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, root.\n\nLet me also think about what \"stable tie-break\" means for the processing order. The instruction says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So at the same depth, sort by node ID (lexicographic).\n\nNow let me also think about edge cases:\n- What if a node we want to remove has its parent's retained status affected? Actually we're tracking `retained` set, so when we remove a node, its parent might then become eligible for removal if all its children are removed.\n\nWait, the algorithm processes nodes in order. If n4 is retained (because it's a required symbol), then n2 can never be removed (it has a retained child n4). But what if n4 were removable and not required? Then we'd process n4 first (deeper), potentially remove it, and then when we get to n2, n4 is no longer in retained, so n2 might be removable.\n\nI think my algorithm handles this correctly since we process deepest first and update `retained` as we go.\n\nLet me also check: when checking \"has retained children\", should this include indirect children or just direct children?\n\n\"a node with retained children\" - I think this means direct children. The reason is that if a child is retained, we can't remove the parent (it would orphan the child or make the tree invalid).\n\nActually in a tree reduction context, removing a node means it and potentially its subtree are removed. But since we process deepest first, by the time we reach a node, its children have already been processed. If a child is still retained (wasn't removed), then the parent can't be removed (it would orphan the child).\n\nMy implementation uses `children[nid]` which are direct children only.\n\nLet me also validate: the root node check. The problem says \"never remove the root\". The root has `removable=false`, so it would be caught by the removable check. But I should also check that required_symbols includes \"root\" potentially... In the fixtures, \"root\" is always in required_symbols. My symbol check would catch it too.\n\nActually, to be safe, I could add an explicit root check, but the existing checks (removable=false and symbol in required_symbols) should cover it.\n\nLet me also think about what to return. The problem says:\n- \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order\"\n- \"Return only JSON-serializable values with the exact named fields\"\n\nI'll return `{\"retained_ids\": [...sorted...], \"removal_order\": [...in order removed...]}`.\n\nLet me write the final implementation:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    # Validate required top-level fields\n    if \"nodes\" not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing 'nodes' field\")\n    if \"required_symbols\" not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing 'required_symbols' field\")\n    \n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    \n    # Validate each node\n    required_node_fields = {\"id\", \"parent\", \"symbol\", \"depth\", \"removable\", \"utility\"}\n    for node in nodes:\n        missing = required_node_fields - set(node.keys())\n        if missing:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Node missing fields: {missing}\")\n    \n    # Build lookup structures\n    node_map = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    \n    # Validate unique IDs\n    if len(node_map) != len(nodes):\n        raise ValueError(\"Duplicate node IDs\")\n    \n    # Build children map\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    \n    # Validate parents and build children\n    roots = []\n    for node in nodes:\n        if node[\"parent\"] is None:\n            roots.append(node[\"id\"])\n        else:\n            if node[\"parent\"] not in node_map:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Parent '{node['parent']}' not found for node '{node['id']}'\")\n            children[node[\"parent\"]].append(node[\"id\"])\n    \n    if len(roots) != 1:\n        raise ValueError(f\"Expected exactly one root, found {len(roots)}\")\n    \n    # Validate that all required symbols exist in the tree\n    all_symbols = {n[\"symbol\"] for n in nodes}\n    for sym in required_symbols:\n        if sym not in all_symbols:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Required symbol '{sym}' not present in tree\")\n    \n    # Track retained nodes\n    retained = set(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes)\n    removal_order = []\n    \n    # Process nodes: deepest first, ties broken by node ID (lexicographic)\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n    \n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node[\"id\"]\n        \n        # Must be removable\n        if not node[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        \n        # Symbol must not be required\n        if node[\"symbol\"] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        \n        # Must have no retained children\n        if any(child in retained for child in children[nid]):\n            continue\n        \n        # After removal, all required symbols must still be represented\n        test_retained = retained - {nid}\n        symbols_after = {node_map[n][\"symbol\"] for n in test_retained}\n        if not required_symbols.issubset(symbols_after):\n            continue\n        \n        # Remove the node\n        retained.discard(nid)\n        removal_order.append(nid)\n    \n    return {\n        \"retained_ids\": sorted(retained),\n        \"removal_order\": removal_order\n    }\n```\n\nThis looks correct to me. Let me trace through all three cases to verify.\n\nCase 0 (already traced): \n- Remove n6, keep everything else\n- retained_ids: [n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, root]\n- removal_order: [n6]\n\nCase 1:\nSame structure. Let me trace:\n- sorted order: n4(d2), n5(d2), n6(d2), n1(d1), n2(d1), n3(d1), root(d0)\n\nn4: symbol=s1, required → skip\nn5: removable=false → skip\nn6: symbol=s0, removable=true, no children\n  - test_retained = {root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5}\n  - symbols = {root, s1, s2, s0} ⊇ {root, s1} → OK\n  - Remove n6\n\nn1: removable=false → skip\nn2: symbol=s2, removable=true\n  - children = [n4], n4 in retained → skip\nn3: removable=false → skip\nroot: removable=false → skip\n\nSame result: remove n6, retain [n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, root]\n\nCase 2:\nSame structure, same result.\n\nThe cases are structurally identical (same tree shape, same removable flags, same symbols), so they all produce the same removal pattern.\n\nNow I need to format this as the required JSON response with the implementation as a JSON string. Let me write the complete response.\n\nThe response must be exactly one JSON object: `{\"implementation\": \"\u003cpython source code as escaped string\u003e\"}`.\n\nLet me write the Python code cleanly and then encode it as a JSON string.\n\nActually, I need to be careful about the JSON encoding. Newlines become `\\n`, backslashes become `\\\\`, quotes become `\\\"`.\n\nLet me write the full Python source and then encode it properly.\n\nThe implementation is straightforward. I'll write it out as a string now.\n\nOne note: I should make sure the response format is pure JSON, no markdown fences, no extra text.\n","type":"thinking"},{"text":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    # Validate required top-level fields\\n    if \\\"nodes\\\" not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing 'nodes' field\\\")\\n    if \\\"required_symbols\\\" not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing 'required_symbols' field\\\")\\n\\n    nodes = case[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    required_symbols = set(case[\\\"required_symbols\\\"])\\n\\n    # Validate each node has required fields\\n    required_node_fields = {\\\"id\\\", \\\"parent\\\", \\\"symbol\\\", \\\"depth\\\", \\\"removable\\\", \\\"utility\\\"}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        missing = required_node_fields - set(node.keys())\\n        if missing:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Node missing fields: {missing}\\\")\\n\\n    # Build lookup structures\\n    node_map = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: n for n in nodes}\\n\\n    # Validate unique IDs\\n    if len(node_map) != len(nodes):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Duplicate node IDs detected\\\")\\n\\n    # Build children map and validate parents\\n    children = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: [] for n in nodes}\\n    roots = []\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        if node[\\\"parent\\\"] is None:\\n            roots.append(node[\\\"id\\\"])\\n        else:\\n            if node[\\\"parent\\\"] not in node_map:\\n                raise ValueError(\\n                    f\\\"Parent '{node['parent']}' not found for node '{node['id']}'\\\"\\n                )\\n            children[node[\\\"parent\\\"]].append(node[\\\"id\\\"])\\n\\n    if len(roots) != 1:\\n        raise ValueError(f\\\"Expected exactly one root node, found {len(roots)}\\\")\\n\\n    # Validate that every required symbol is present in the tree\\n    all_symbols = {n[\\\"symbol\\\"] for n in nodes}\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        if sym not in all_symbols:\\n            raise ValueError(\\n                f\\\"Required symbol '{sym}' is not represented by any node\\\"\\n            )\\n\\n    # Track retained set (starts as all nodes)\\n    retained = set(n[\\\"id\\\"] for n in nodes)\\n    removal_order = []\\n\\n    # Process candidates: deepest first; stable tie-break by node ID (lexicographic)\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\\\"depth\\\"], n[\\\"id\\\"]))\\n\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node[\\\"id\\\"]\\n\\n        # Rule 1: node must be marked removable\\n        if not node[\\\"removable\\\"]:\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Rule 2: never remove a node whose symbol is required\\n        if node[\\\"symbol\\\"] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Rule 3: never remove a node that still has retained children\\n        if any(child in retained for child in children[nid]):\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Rule 4: after tentative removal, every required symbol must still\\n        # be represented by at least one retained node\\n        test_retained = retained - {nid}\\n        symbols_after = {node_map[n][\\\"symbol\\\"] for n in test_retained}\\n        if not required_symbols.issubset(symbols_after):\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Commit removal\\n        retained.discard(nid)\\n        removal_order.append(nid)\\n\\n    return {\\n        \\\"retained_ids\\\": sorted(retained),\\n        \\\"removal_order\\\": removal_order\\n    }\\n\"}","type":"text"}],"id":"msg_01lmRcf6g6QdiB2M7scCfBml","model":"claude-opus-4-7","role":"assistant","stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":2191,"inference_geo":"not_available","input_tokens":56,"output_tokens":4946,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":4089},"service_tier":"standard"}}